  "content": {
    "id": "p24-cc8-10-group",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "True or False?",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p24-cc8-10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "In scenarios where the ambiguous case arises, it is always the case that both the original and the ambiguous cases are valid.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. A supplementary candidate is invalid if its sum with the given angle is at least $180^\\circ$. Both candidates must be checked.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-cc8-10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "The diagram in a sine-rule problem may suggest an obtuse angle that fails the triangle angle-sum test, while the acute candidate gives a valid triangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. A diagram may suggest an obtuse angle that fails the angle-sum check, while its acute supplementary candidate gives a valid triangle. Check both candidates against the given data.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-cc8-10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "If one possible triangle for two given sides and a non-included angle is right-angled, it is always the only solution.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. One possible triangle can be right-angled while another is not. For example, take $A=30^\\circ$, $a=1$ and $b=\\sqrt3$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin B&=\\frac{\\sqrt3\\sin30^\\circ}{1}\\\\\\sin B&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\\\B&=60^\\circ\\text{ or }120^\\circ\\\\C&=90^\\circ\\text{ or }30^\\circ\\end{align*}$$\nBoth triangles are valid. If a right angle is explicitly given as a condition, only the right-angled configuration is permitted.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
